 ],
 "literal": "If medicine, she is medicine; if treasure, she is treasure / having sprouted beauty-spots, beautiful, lofty, young breasts, / large shoulders, and a slender waist, / the daughter of the forest-dwellers of the rocky mountains.",
 "translation": "If she is medicine, she is indeed medicine. If she is a treasure, she is indeed a treasure—the simple daughter of the forest-dwellers of the rocky mountains, who has large shoulders, a slender waist, and beautiful, lofty, young breasts covered with newly sprouted beauty-spots.",
 "modern": "மலைகள் நிறைந்த காட்டில் வாழும் வேடர்களின் எளிய மகளான அவள், உடம்பில் அரும்பிய தேமலும், அழகிய பருத்த இளமையான மார்பகங்களும், பெரிய தோள்களும், மெல்லிய இடையும் கொண்டவள். நோய் தீர்க்கும் மருந்து என்று சொன்னால் அவள் மருந்தாவாள்; சேமித்து வைத்த செல்வம் என்று சொன்னால் அவள் செல்வமாவாள்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the hero in the Pālai (wasteland) landscape, expressing his deep love and admiration for the heroine. He describes her as the simple daughter of the mountain forest-dwellers (kāṉavar) who live among the rocks. He praises her physical beauty, noting her large shoulders (peruntōḷ), slender waist (nusuppu), and young breasts adorned with newly sprouted beauty-spots (cuṇaṅku). To him, she is both a healing medicine (maruntu) for his love-sickness and a precious treasure (vaippu) to be guarded. The contrast between her humble forest origin and her immense value to the hero highlights the intensity of his devotion.",
